SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"World Bank Deepens Commitment to Building Human Capital and Inclusive Development in the Democratic Republic of Congo","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-02-22","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/02/22/world-bank-deepens-commitment-to-building-human-capital-and-inclusive-development-in-the-democratic-republic-of-congo","claim":"The World Bank approved a 2022-2026 partnership framework for the DRC that put strong emphasis on human development, improved basic services, governance, and stabilization.","locator":"lines 146-148","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct World Bank press release with explicit country-program priorities and date.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 target-period policy and baseline context.","ruler_attribution":"Moderate: it reflects the incumbent government's strategic priorities, but not a personally authored social policy outcome.","contrary_evidence":["The same release says poverty is rising and governance remains weak."],"lenses":["6B.1","6B.9","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"The World Bank Supports Free Primary Education and Maternal and Child Health Improvements in the DRC","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2020-06-15","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2020/06/15/the-world-bank-supports-free-primary-education-and-maternal-and-child-health-improvements-in-the-drc","claim":"The World Bank said the education project would help roll out free primary education, support more than nine million children to re-enroll and stay in school, and expand access for poor children excluded from schooling.","locator":"lines 151-157","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary World Bank release with concrete program design and expected reach.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Pre-2022 baseline that remained relevant in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Moderate: the project supports Tshisekedi-era policy, but delivery depended on implementers and schools.","contrary_evidence":["The same release implies many children were still excluded and schools were reopening from lockdown."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.4","6B.9","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Child survival","publisher":"UNICEF","publication_date":"2022-01-01","url":"https://www.unicef.org/drcongo/en/what-we-do/child-survival","claim":"UNICEF said many DRC children still die before age five from preventable diseases and that health delivery is hindered by stock-outs, underpaid staff, poor quality care, and limited financial access.","locator":"lines 79-80","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UNICEF country program page with explicit service-delivery diagnosis.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Long-running baseline, still applicable in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Moderate: this is national service delivery under the incumbent government, but not uniquely ruler-caused.","contrary_evidence":["The same page also says UNICEF is working with partners to vaccinate children and reduce child mortality."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.4","6B.6","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Emergency response","publisher":"UNICEF","publication_date":"2022-01-01","url":"https://www.unicef.org/drcongo/en/what-we-do/emergency-response","claim":"UNICEF said that in 2022 the DRC faced two Ebola epidemics and that more than 1.3 million under-5 children needed treatment for severe wasting, 6.6 million children needed emergency water and sanitation, and 2.7 million children needed emergency education support.","locator":"lines 78-80","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct UNICEF country page with a quantified humanitarian needs estimate.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 target-year humanitarian context.","ruler_attribution":"Moderate: these needs are partly structural and conflict-driven, but they frame incumbent responsibility for response.","contrary_evidence":["The same page also describes UNICEF’s ongoing response efforts."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.6","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Safe water for students and families in Walungu","publisher":"UNICEF","publication_date":"2022-12-05","url":"https://www.unicef.org/drcongo/en/recits/safe-water-students-residents-walungu","claim":"UNICEF reported that it had connected a South Kivu primary school to safe drinking water and installed hygienic toilets, improving hygiene and school conditions.","locator":"lines 73-75","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct field story with concrete service-delivery details and date.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 target-period delivery evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Moderate: a UNICEF-supported local service intervention, not a uniquely presidential act.","contrary_evidence":["The story implies the school previously lacked water and children had to fetch it elsewhere."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.4","6B.8"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Improving girls’ access to education in Kasai","publisher":"UNICEF","publication_date":"2022-09-19","url":"https://www.unicef.org/drcongo/en/recits/improving-girls-access-education-Kasai","claim":"UNICEF said that in Kasai only 12 girls out of 100 boys attend lower secondary school and that it was running a girls-education pilot with new classrooms, sanitation, and boreholes.","locator":"lines 59-72","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct UNICEF field report with explicit gender-gap and intervention details.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 target-period education evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Moderate: the project addresses a government-relevant gap, but delivery is mainly partner-led.","contrary_evidence":["The same story shows a severe preexisting gender gap and early marriage pressures."],"lenses":["6B.3","6B.8","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Education Cannot Wait and UNICEF with partners call for substantial increase in funding for crisis-affected children in the Democratic Republic of the Congo","publisher":"UNICEF","publication_date":"2022-10-26","url":"https://www.unicef.org/drcongo/en/press-releases/call-substantial-increase-funding-crisis-affected-children","claim":"UNICEF said 3.2 million children aged 6 to 11 were out of school, rural completion was far below urban completion, and only 38 percent of primary schools had access to water.","locator":"lines 91-95","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct UNICEF/ECW press release with quantified access and equity gaps.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 target-period evidence, even though some figures use earlier survey data.","ruler_attribution":"Moderate: this is a 2022 state-of-service baseline under the incumbent government, but not a pure ruler-attributable outcome.","contrary_evidence":["The same release also describes a funded resilience programme and government collaboration."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.5","6B.9","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"The dilemma of schools that shelter DRC’s displaced families","publisher":"UNICEF","publication_date":"2023-09-02","url":"https://www.unicef.org/drcongo/en/stories/dilemma-schools-shelter-drcs-displaced-families","claim":"UNICEF said more than 2,000 schools had closed since January 2022 because of insecurity and displacement, depriving children of education.","locator":"lines 57-60","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct UNICEF field story with a clear quantified disruption and date.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly spans the 2022 target year and its consequences.","ruler_attribution":"Moderate: the closures are driven by conflict and displacement, but the national government bears response responsibility.","contrary_evidence":["The same story shows UNICEF and partners building temporary learning spaces where possible."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.6","6B.8","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"The impact of the cash transfer intervention in the commune of Nsele in Kinshasa","publisher":"UNICEF","publication_date":"2024-04-01","url":"https://www.unicef.org/drcongo/en/report/impact-cash-transfer-intervention-commune-nsele","claim":"UNICEF said the cash-transfer intervention reached about 23,000 households initially and then about 16,000 of the most vulnerable households in the social-protection phase, with complementary training on women’s leadership, rights, and financial management.","locator":"lines 70-71","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Impact report with concrete target population and programme content.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective evidence about a programme active in the target period.","ruler_attribution":"Moderate: the programme was delivered with government-facing targeting lessons, but implementation was partner-led.","contrary_evidence":["The same report later says the evaluation found only limited impacts on food security and dietary diversity."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.7","6B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"The impact of the cash transfer intervention in the commune of Nsele in Kinshasa","publisher":"UNICEF","publication_date":"2024-04-01","url":"https://www.unicef.org/drcongo/en/report/impact-cash-transfer-intervention-commune-nsele","claim":"UNICEF reported that the quantitative evaluation found limited impacts on household food security, resilience coping, and dietary diversity, and that implementation problems needed correction before scaling up.","locator":"lines 72-73","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Impact evaluation with explicit negative findings and implementation diagnosis.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective assessment of a 2022-relevant intervention.","ruler_attribution":"Moderate: this is about a government-supported social protection effort, but the weakness is mainly implementation-level.","contrary_evidence":["The qualitative interviews were more positive and the report notes lessons for future targeting."],"lenses":["6B.5","6B.7","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Lancement de la 3eme phase du projet conjoint de transferts monétaires humanitaires aux populations vulnérables à Kinshasa","publisher":"UNICEF","publication_date":"2022-03-01","url":"https://www.unicef.org/drcongo/communiques-presse/lancement-3eme-phase-projet-transferts-monetaires","claim":"UNICEF said the third phase of the Kinshasa cash-transfer project targeted nearly 4,000 vulnerable households, including pregnant women, children under five, disabled heads of household, and elderly people.","locator":"lines 67-70","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct program announcement with clear vulnerable-group targeting.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 target-period social-protection evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Moderate: government collaboration is explicit, but implementation is partner-led.","contrary_evidence":["The note also says the programme was a continuation of earlier COVID-related response phases."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.7","6B.8"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Children’s education, a priority for Marlène","publisher":"UNICEF","publication_date":"2022-10-10","url":"https://www.unicef.org/drcongo/en/recits/childrens-education-a-priority-for-Marlene","claim":"UNICEF said more than 4,000 vulnerable families in N’Sele received unconditional cash transfers that helped them care for families and educate children while improving access to basic social services.","locator":"lines 80-80","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct beneficiary story with quantified reach and concrete uses of assistance.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 target-period evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Moderate: the benefit is real but delivered through a donor-supported programme, not a uniquely presidential policy choice.","contrary_evidence":["The story is anecdotal and does not establish national-scale effects."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.8"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"UNICEF and partners respond to unprecedented cholera outbreak in Sankuru, DR Congo","publisher":"UNICEF","publication_date":"2022-09-01","url":"https://www.unicef.org/drcongo/en/press-release/unicef-partners-respond-unprecedented-cholera-outbreak","claim":"UNICEF said more than 30,000 people, including more than 17,000 children, were threatened by a cholera outbreak in Sankuru and that 24 people had died, including 8 children.","locator":"lines 64-65","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct UNICEF emergency release with specific caseload and mortality figures.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 target-period crisis evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Moderate: outbreak response reflects state capacity, though the event itself is not ruler-caused.","contrary_evidence":["UNICEF says health, water and sanitation services were being deployed to respond."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.6"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Working with communities to curb cholera in the Democratic Republic of the Congo","publisher":"WHO Regional Office for Africa","publication_date":"2022-11-25","url":"https://www.afro.who.int/countries/democratic-republic-of-congo/news/working-communities-curb-cholera-democratic-republic-congo","claim":"WHO said that by late 2022 the DRC had reported more than 12,300 suspected cholera cases and 222 deaths in 17 provinces.","locator":"line 61","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct WHO regional office publication with a quantified national outbreak snapshot.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 target-period health crisis evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Moderate: this is a national public-health crisis under the incumbent government.","contrary_evidence":["WHO frames this as an outbreak control effort and discusses community response."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.6"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"The Democratic Republic of the Congo declares Ebola resurgence in North Kivu","publisher":"WHO Regional Office for Africa","publication_date":"2022-08-23","url":"https://www.afro.who.int/countries/democratic-republic-of-congo/news/democratic-republic-congo-declares-ebola-resurgence-north-kivu","claim":"WHO reported the 2022 Ebola resurgence in North Kivu and said a prior flare-up had been brought under control in about two months with 11 cases and 6 deaths.","locator":"lines 205-207","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct WHO outbreak report with dates and case/death counts.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 target-period crisis evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Moderate: outbreak control was a state responsibility under Tshisekedi's administration.","contrary_evidence":["WHO emphasizes vaccination and surveillance as the response mechanism."],"lenses":["6B.6","6B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"WHO Global Health Emergency Appeal: DRC","publisher":"WHO","publication_date":"2022-03-07","url":"https://www.who.int/publications/m/item/who-global-health-emergency-appeal-drc","claim":"WHO said that during vaccine responses in the DRC, 2,635,401 people were vaccinated against cholera, 162,518 against meningitis, and 1,180,268 children against measles.","locator":"line 224","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary WHO publication with explicit vaccination delivery figures.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 target-period emergency-response evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Moderate: these are government-implemented public-health responses supported by WHO.","contrary_evidence":["The appeal also says the country remains in a high-grade emergency with many outbreaks."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.5","6B.6"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"UNHCR deeply concerned by renewed violence displacing thousands in North Kivu, DR Congo","publisher":"UNHCR","publication_date":"2022-05-13","url":"https://www.unhcr.org/news/briefing-notes/unhcr-deeply-concerned-renewed-violence-displacing-thousands-north-kivu-dr","claim":"UNHCR said more than 72,000 people had been displaced by fighting in North Kivu and that displaced families were struggling to access food and clean water.","locator":"lines 392-397","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct UNHCR briefing note with displacement and welfare consequences.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 target-period humanitarian evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Moderate: displacement is driven by conflict, but response and protection are national-government responsibilities.","contrary_evidence":["UNHCR notes some emergency assistance and use of temporary shelters."],"lenses":["6B.3","6B.6","6B.8","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Democratic Republic of the Congo Country Strategic Plan (2021–2026)","publisher":"World Food Programme","publication_date":"2021-01-01","url":"https://www.wfp.org/operations/cd02-democratic-republic-congo-country-strategic-plan-2021-2025","claim":"WFP said food and nutrition security in the DRC is hit by conflict, epidemics, climate events, weak infrastructure, and underinvestment in agriculture, health, and human capital.","locator":"lines 268-271","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary WFP country strategy with a plain-language diagnosis of constraints.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Baseline and policy context for 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Moderate: this is a structural baseline, not purely ruler-caused, but it frames the incumbent's constraints.","contrary_evidence":["The same plan says WFP supports government priorities rather than substituting for them."],"lenses":["6B.1","6B.2","6B.4","6B.9","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Democratic Republic of the Congo: Acute Food Insecurity Situation July - December 2022 and Projection for January - June 2023","publisher":"IPC - Integrated Food Security Phase Classification","publication_date":"2022-10-18","url":"https://www.ipcinfo.org/ipc-country-analysis/details-map/en/c/1155972/?iso3=COD","claim":"IPC estimated that 26.4 million people in the DRC were facing acute food insecurity in mid- to late 2022, driven by poverty, conflict, displacement, low agricultural production, high food prices, and weak infrastructure.","locator":"lines 80-85","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary IPC national analysis with quantified food insecurity and causal factors.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 target-period welfare evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Moderate: the causes are mixed, but the government is responsible for response and mitigation.","contrary_evidence":["IPC also notes a slight improvement in the 2023 projection compared with late 2022."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.6","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Democratic Republic of the Congo - Data in Emergencies (DIEM) Monitoring System – Household Survey – Round 4, Democratic Republic of the Congo, 2022","publisher":"FAO Microdata","publication_date":"2022-09-01","url":"https://microdata.fao.org/index.php/catalog/2507","claim":"FAO said it ran a nationally representative 2022 household survey across 11 provinces to assess livelihoods, shocks, food security, consumption, and assistance needs.","locator":"lines 83-106 and 133-137","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary FAO microdata catalogue entry with design and coverage details.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 target-period survey evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Moderate: this is measurement infrastructure rather than a ruler action, but it is strong evidence on the information environment.","contrary_evidence":["The survey scope is only 11 of 26 provinces, so it is not a perfect national census."],"lenses":["6B.5","6B.6","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Strengthening resilience in the face of the COVID-19 pandemic and contributing to poverty reduction in peri-urban Kinshasa through agricultural livelihoods support","publisher":"FAO","publication_date":"2022-11-29","url":"https://www.fao.org/evaluation/highlights/detail/covid-kinshasa/en","claim":"FAO reported that its Kinshasa COVID-response project aimed to strengthen the socioeconomic and nutritional situation of 2,550 affected households and to stabilize income, food access, and dietary diversification.","locator":"lines 51-55 and 63-68","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"FAO evaluation highlight with explicit target population, objectives, and delivery mechanisms.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 target-period crisis-response evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Moderate: the project was implemented with government collaboration, but it was not a purely presidential initiative.","contrary_evidence":["FAO says COVID-19 had already reduced incomes and food security in low-income households."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.6","6B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Providing safety nets for the most vulnerable in the Democratic Republic of Congo: Incremental steps with a large footprint","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2023-02-10","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/results/2023/02/10/providing-safety-nets-for-the-most-vulnerable-in-the-democratic-republic-of-congo-incremental-steps-with-a-large-footpri","claim":"The STEP social-protection program benefited over 5.8 million people and supported 530,000 individuals with cash transfers, cash-for-work, investment grants, and training between 2014 and December 2022.","locator":"lines 66-67","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"World Bank results page with cumulative outcomes and dates through the target year.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective through end-2022 evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Moderate: the programme is government-linked but donor-implemented, so attribution is shared.","contrary_evidence":["The same page makes clear this is an incremental safety-net effort rather than a comprehensive national system."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.9","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Empowering Girls and Enhancing Learning in the Democratic Republic of Congo","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2023-05-18","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/results/2023/05/18/empowering-girls-and-enhancing-learning-in-the-democratic-republic-of-afe-congo","claim":"The World Bank said the DRC had significant gender gaps in school attendance and learning outcomes, and that the resulting analysis helped design the Girls' Learning and Empowerment Project.","locator":"lines 69-80","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"World Bank results page with explicit link from evidence to policy design.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective evidence about 2022-era policy design.","ruler_attribution":"Moderate: the Ministry of Finance and education authorities are involved, but this is still donor-led analysis.","contrary_evidence":["The page shows the baseline problem was severe in 2017 and needed targeted intervention."],"lenses":["6B.3","6B.5","6B.8","6B.9","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Spotlight on basic education completion and foundational learning: Democratic Republic of Congo","publisher":"UNESCO IICBA","publication_date":"2022-01-01","url":"https://www.iicba.unesco.org/en/africa-education-knowledge-platform/spotlight-basic-education-completion-and-foundational-learning-democratic-republic-congo","claim":"UNESCO reported that basic education access expanded since 2016, but net enrolment was still only 69 percent, quality was weak because of underfunding and poor infrastructure, and 73 percent of Grade 6 students fell below minimum reading benchmarks.","locator":"lines 197-198","source_type":"scholarship","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UNESCO knowledge-platform report summarizing a national education diagnosis with specific indicators.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Baseline and 2022 educational context.","ruler_attribution":"Moderate: this is a national education baseline rather than a pure ruler effect, but it frames feasible improvement.","contrary_evidence":["The same report says access had expanded, so the picture is mixed rather than uniformly negative."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.4","6B.5","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Inclusive Governance Initiative Democratic Republic of the Congo Baseline Report","publisher":"United Nations Development Programme","publication_date":"2022-03-29","url":"https://www.undp.org/drcongo/publications/inclusive-governance-initiative-democratic-republic-congo-baseline-report","claim":"UNDP said the project sought to make state entities more accountable to the whole population, including LGBTI people, and to support better laws, more responsive public services, and rights-affirming social norms.","locator":"lines 32-35","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UNDP baseline report with clear human-rights and service-delivery objectives.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 baseline and policy context.","ruler_attribution":"Moderate: the report is about state accountability and inclusion, but it is a project baseline rather than an outcome evaluation.","contrary_evidence":["The same report is only a baseline assessment, so it does not prove delivery."],"lenses":["6B.3","6B.8","6B.9"]}
Search ledger: candidates found about 60, documents opened 33, duplicate families removed about 20, irrelevant or out-of-period items about 10, inaccessible items about 5, accepted distinct URLs about 25.

Material gaps and next best directions:
- Direct nationally representative 2022 household data on realized welfare change under Tshisekedi is still thin. The best next step is the 2022/2023 DHS or MICS tables from INS, plus any poverty/consumption microdata.
- I still want cleaner 2022 budget-execution or audit evidence on whether health, education, WASH, and social-protection appropriations were actually spent and on time.
- Attribution remains mixed for many welfare gains because a large share of the visible evidence comes from UNICEF, World Bank, WFP, FAO, WHO, UNHCR, and IPC programmes rather than direct presidential action.
- Best next sources: Ministry of Budget execution reports, Cour des comptes audits, INS household surveys, and project completion/evaluation reports for EESSE, PDSS, and STEP.
